# Fleet Bundle

Generated by AICR v{{ .BundlerVersion }}

## Prerequisites

- Rancher with Fleet (HelmOps and GitRepos are part of every Rancher install)
- `kubectl` configured for the Rancher management (local) cluster
{{- if not .IsHelmOp }}
- A Git repository Fleet can read
{{- end }}

## Bundles

Fleet reconciles the bundles in this order; each one `dependsOn` the previous
bundle and starts only after it is `Ready`.

| Bundle | Release | Namespace | Version |
|--------|---------|-----------|---------|
{{- range .Components }}
| {{ .Bundle }} | {{ .Release }} | {{ .Namespace }} | {{ .Version }} |
{{- end }}

{{ if .UpgradeNotice }}{{ .UpgradeNotice }}{{ end -}}
{{ if .IsHelmOp }}## Deployment

1. Apply the HelmOps to the Fleet workspace `{{ .Namespace }}` on the Rancher
   management cluster. No Git repository is needed: Fleet pulls each chart
   from its repository, with the values inlined in `helmops.yaml`.

   ```bash
   kubectl apply -f helmops.yaml
   ```
{{ if .IsLocal }}
   The HelmOps target the Rancher local cluster.
{{ else }}
2. Opt clusters in. Nothing is deployed until a cluster carries the label
   the HelmOps select on:

   ```bash
   kubectl label clusters.fleet.cattle.io -n {{ .Namespace }} <cluster> {{ .BundleLabel }}={{ .AppName }}
   ```
{{ end }}
{{ else }}## Deployment

1. Push this directory to the repository and branch named in `gitrepo.yaml`
   (edit `spec.repo` / `spec.branch` if you did not pass `--repo`). If the
   bundle lives in a subdirectory of the repository, prefix every entry in
   `spec.paths` with that subdirectory.

2. Apply the GitRepo to the Fleet workspace `{{ .Namespace }}`:

   ```bash
   kubectl apply -f gitrepo.yaml
   ```
{{ if .IsLocal }}
   The GitRepo targets the Rancher local cluster.
{{ else }}
3. Opt clusters in. Nothing is deployed until a cluster carries the label
   the GitRepo selects on:

   ```bash
   kubectl label clusters.fleet.cattle.io -n {{ .Namespace }} <cluster> {{ .BundleLabel }}={{ .AppName }}
   ```
{{ end }}
{{ end }}Monitor progress in Rancher under **Continuous Delivery**, or:

```bash
kubectl get bundles -n {{ .Namespace }} -l {{ .BundleLabel }}={{ .AppName }}
```

If a release with the same name already exists in the target namespace (for
example a GPU Operator installed with `helm install gpu-operator`), Fleet
upgrades it in place. Objects owned by a release with a different name are
not adopted: the install fails on the ownership conflict instead, so remove
or rename that release first.
{{ if .HasDynamic }}
### Per-cluster customization

{{ if .IsHelmOp -}}
Releases with dynamic value paths carry those values inline in
`helmops.yaml` (the `cluster-values.yaml` files are kept for reference and are
not read by Fleet). Edit the values under each HelmOp's `spec.helm.values`
before applying, or regenerate the bundle with the values you need. To vary
values per cluster, set `helm.values` on individual entries of that HelmOp's
`spec.targets`.
{{- else -}}
Releases with dynamic value paths have a `cluster-values.yaml` next to
`values.yaml`; edit it before pushing. To vary values per cluster, add
`targetCustomizations` to that folder's `fleet.yaml`.
{{- end }}
{{ end }}{{ if .HasVendored }}
### Vendored charts

This bundle carries vendored Helm charts. Fleet stores each bundle as a
Kubernetes object, so keep every folder well under the ~1MiB etcd object limit.
{{ end }}
## Removal

```bash
kubectl delete -f {{ if .IsHelmOp }}helmops.yaml{{ else }}gitrepo.yaml{{ end }}
```

Fleet uninstalls every release in the bundle.

## Limitations

- Fleet installs charts with Helm, which creates a chart's `crds/` on first
  install but never upgrades them.{{ if .CRDOwners }} These components own
  their CRDs, and the helm deployer refreshes them before every upgrade:
{{ range .CRDOwners }}
  - {{ . }}
{{- end }}

  Before upgrading any of them, apply the new chart's CRDs with the CRD-apply
  command under "Upgrade, uninstall, and troubleshooting" in the AICR
  component catalog (`docs/user/component-catalog.md`), then let Fleet
  reconcile.{{ end }}
- Readiness hooks (`--readiness-hooks`) are not supported.
- A chart that ships CRDs in `crds/`, templates objects of those kinds, and
  uses Helm `lookup` fails Fleet's server-side dry run on a cluster where the
  CRDs do not exist yet. Apply that chart's CRDs first.
